gc.c: every GC object slot a Sublet child lifetime of its heap page, under MRB_CAPSTONE_SUBLET (4.0.0-rc2).

Two instructions do the work. CDERIVE makes a child capability of a
non-linear parent, bounded to a sub-range; CREVOKE(object) ends the
object's lifetime and everything derived inside it.

Each heap page keeps, per slot, the child it handed out. mrb_obj_alloc hands
out the object as a child of the page bounded to its slot, a CDERIVE of the
page's own pointer, and registers that child, not the GC's own pointer, in
the GC arena. The sweep, and free_heap at mrb_close, revoke the child once
obj_free has freed the object, so every stale copy of the object's pointer
is dead before the slot is reused. Freeing a page frees its malloc'd object,
below which every child lies.

The GC works on its own pointers, derived from the page. Such a pointer
names the page, the parent of every child, not the object's lifetime, so
gc_retire gives CREVOKE the child the page kept for the slot. Where the GC
is asked about a pointer that may be dead -- mrb_object_dead_p, and
obj_free's walk of a dying fiber's frames, whose envs may have been swept
earlier in the same sweep -- it finds the slot by address and reads it
through its own pointer. gc_each_objects hands each live object to its
callback as the object's child.

Without the define the preprocessed file is unchanged. Needs
capstone/runtime/include on the include path (capstone/capability.h) and the
virtual profile.

